        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every "self-propelled" blower drives its wheels</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many single-stage machines are only auger-assisted. They pull well on bare pavement but will not climb a slope or push through a plow berm on their own. Read the spec sheet for a transmission, forward speeds or a drive control before you pay for something you think is powered drive but is not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying tracks for a flat paved driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Track drive is excellent on slopes, ice and gravel, but on a level paved driveway it mostly adds cost, weight and slower turning. A wheeled two-stage machine with optional chains handles that surface well and is easier to maneuver in a crowded garage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the drive too fast in heavy snow</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A higher forward speed feels productive but overloads the auger and impeller in wet or deep snow, which clogs the chute and strains belts. Slow down so the machine can process what it takes in; you will usually clear the driveway faster overall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping drive belt and friction disc checks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Driven machines rely on belts, cables and, on many models, a friction disc that wears. Owners often ignore these until the drive slips mid-storm. Inspect and adjust them during the fall service as the owner's manual describes, and keep a spare belt on hand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring steering on a curved driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Shoppers compare engine size and clearing width and forget that a two-stage machine with locked wheels is hard to turn. If your driveway curves or you clear tight paths, trigger steering will make a larger difference to your effort than a slightly bigger engine.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a single-stage snow blower self-propelled?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not in the true sense. Most single-stage machines are auger-assisted: the rubber-edged auger touches the pavement and pulls the machine forward. That works well on flat, bare pavement but fades on slopes, in deep snow or when the auger is lifted. A few single-stage hybrids add a real drive system, so check the spec sheet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are all two-stage snow blowers self-propelled?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Nearly all two-stage models sold for home use have driven wheels or tracks, because the auger sits above the ground and cannot pull the machine. The differences between them are in the drive type, the number of speeds, whether they offer trigger steering, and whether they ride on wheels or tracks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a self-propelled snow blower worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth it if you have a slope, a long or curved driveway, heavy wet snow, a regular plow berm, or limited strength. On a short, flat, paved driveway with light snow, a single-stage machine's auger pull is usually enough and the extra cost and weight of a driven machine buy little.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between wheel drive and track drive?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wheel drive uses two large lugged tires and is quicker to turn, cheaper and simpler. Track drive uses rubber tracks that spread weight and grip better on steep slopes, ice and packed snow. Tracks cost more and turn more slowly, so they suit hillside and icy properties rather than flat driveways.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do battery snow blowers have self-propelled drive?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some do. Battery two-stage machines such as EGO's 24-inch model and Toro's Power Max e24 are sold with driven wheels and run on two batteries. Most battery single-stage blowers rely on auger pull instead. The drive shares battery power with the auger, so expect shorter runtime on slopes and in heavy snow.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is trigger steering on a snow blower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Trigger steering, also sold as power steering, adds a trigger under each handle that disengages drive to one wheel. The machine then pivots around that wheel with little effort. It makes driven two-stage blowers much easier to turn at the end of each pass, which matters most on curved or crowded driveways.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What maintenance does a self-propelled drive need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Expect to inspect and adjust drive belts and cables, and on friction-disc machines, check the disc for wear and keep it dry and free of oil. Tracked models need track tension checks. Most manuals include these in the seasonal service, and fall is the best time to do them before the first storm.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

