        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A self-propelled snow blower is worth paying for when the drive does work you would otherwise do yourself: climbing a slope, pushing into a plow berm, covering a long driveway or protecting a back that should not be shoving a heavy machine. In those situations, a driven two-stage blower, with tracks on steep or icy ground and trigger steering on curved drives, is the right tool.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            On a short, flat, paved driveway with light snow, a single-stage machine's auger pull does the job for less money and less upkeep. Decide based on your driveway and your strength, not the word on the box, and compare the specific models on our <a href="/best-snow-blower/">best snow blower list</a>.
          </p>
